INT. SUBURBAN KITCHEN - NIGHT

Dark. Candles on the counter. A camping lantern hisses on the table.

SAL (V.O.)
Power went out at eight-fifteen. By nine, the whole street was in somebody's kitchen. That's how it works out here.

SAL (40s, cardigan, tea towel on shoulder) stirs a pot on a gas ring, while KAY (60s, his mother-in-law) cuts bread at the table, TEDDY (9) kneels on a chair building a wobbly tower of mugs, and a golden retriever sleeps with its chin on Sal's slipper. Through the window, two teenagers from next door shine a phone torch over the fence, whispering and giggling.

BIG DAVE (50s, huge, soaked) appears in the back doorway holding a bag of ice.

BIG DAVE
Freezer's gone at ours. Is yours alright?

SAL
Come in, Dave, you're letting the heat out.

KAY
There's no heat, Sal.

SAL
Then you're letting the cold out.

Teddy's tower collapses. The dog lifts its head, sighs, resumes sleeping.

TEDDY
It wasn't me!

KAY
It's never you.

BIG DAVE
Who's the lady out front?

SAL
What lady?

BIG DAVE
Standing on your drive. Umbrella. Been there ten minutes.

They look. Through the window: a figure at the end of the path, still, umbrella dripping.

SAL (V.O.)
I didn't know her. That's all I'll say about that.

KAY
She looks lost.

TEDDY
She looks cold.

SAL
Teddy, stay put.

Sal opens the front door. The Woman (50s, neat grey coat) steps in out of the rain, folds her umbrella.

THE WOMAN
I'm sorry. Is this number fourteen? My sister's number fourteen.

SAL
This is twelve.

THE WOMAN
Oh. Ruth Aldana. Ruth. I'm so sorry.

KAY
Ruth! Sit down, Ruth. Soup?

BIG DAVE
Fourteen's the one with the gnome.

RUTH
That's her.

TEDDY
We've got bread.

SAL (V.O.)
There's no manual for strangers on a night like this. You just pull out another chair.

Ruth sits. The candle gutters and holds. The dog thumps its tail once on the lino.
